WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.861 --> 00:00:05.702
What hotel must be rebuilt every year?

2
00:00:06.243 --> 00:00:07.423
What church can you eat?

3
00:00:08.044 --> 00:00:10.365
What kind of house is made of soda bottles?

4
00:00:11.125 --> 00:00:17.148
The Ice Hotel in Sweden must be rebuilt every year because it meltsㅡit's made entirely from ice.

5
00:00:17.988 --> 00:00:23.771
The church in the Wieliczka salt mine in Poland can be eaten, although it is not recommended, because

6
00:00:23.831 --> 00:00:25.652
it is carved entirely from salt.

7
00:00:26.532 --> 00:00:30.895
In Spain, Brazil, and the United States, people have made houses from bottles.

8
00:00:31.735 --> 00:00:37.458
When someone drinks a soda and recycles the bottle, that bottle may become a part of an environmentally

9
00:00:37.518 --> 00:00:39.239
friendly building, the bottle house.

10
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.702
These are just a few examples of the unique buildings that we can find around the world.

11
00:00:45.703 --> 00:00:52.006
The Ice Hotel of Sweden Some people prefer to vacation in the sun, but the Ice Hotel has

12
00:00:52.046 --> 00:00:55.128
people checking in and chilling out at sub-zero temperatures.

13
00:00:55.964 --> 00:01:00.087
The Ice Hotel has over sixty rooms, a bar, a cinema, and a church.

14
00:01:00.968 --> 00:01:03.649
It is built from thousands of tons of snow and ice.

15
00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:10.254
The Ice Hotel lies two hundred kilometers north of the Arctic Circle, near the town of Jukkasjarvi, on

16
00:01:10.294 --> 00:01:12.255
the banks of the Torne River in Sweden.

17
00:01:13.196 --> 00:01:19.220
Before the first Ice Hotel was built, very few tourists came to Jukkasjarvi during the long, cold, and

18
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.101
snowy winters.

19
00:01:20.962 --> 00:01:24.224
For two weeks of the year in December, there is total darkness.

20
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.062
In 1990, a local adventure-tourism company decided to treat the town's cold winter as an asset.

21
00:01:31.642 --> 00:01:36.823
The company built a large igloo in the shape of a cylinder and held art exhibitions there.

22
00:01:37.623 --> 00:01:41.944
The "igloo gallery" was successful and sparked the idea for an ice hotel.

23
00:01:43.165 --> 00:01:46.626
Some guests visiting the gallery fell asleep there by mistake.

24
00:01:47.446 --> 00:01:53.588
They had reindeer skins to keep them warm, and when they awoke, they felt refreshed, well rested, and,

25
00:01:53.628 --> 00:01:54.888
most surprisingly, warm!

26
00:01:55.792 --> 00:02:01.836
Inspired by these guests' experiences, the tourism company built the first Ice Hotel in 1992.

27
00:02:02.556 --> 00:02:04.678
Since then, it has been rebuilt annually.

28
00:02:04.898 --> 00:02:09.220
Around thirty local artists and builders start working on the structure each October.

29
00:02:09.981 --> 00:02:14.664
They carve blocks of ice that will make up the foundation, walls, and roof of the hotel.

30
00:02:15.364 --> 00:02:20.947
The ice is crystal clear because it all comes from the Torne River, a mountain river with clean

31
00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:21.628
spring water.

32
00:02:22.432 --> 00:02:28.076
By the beginning of December, the main building is almost finished, and work on the interior begins.

33
00:02:29.057 --> 00:02:35.081
Sculptors cut and shape the ice to create windows, beds, tables, chairs, lamps, and decorative sculptures.

34
00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:42.126
The first guests arrive in mid-December, although the sculptors' work inside continues until January.

35
00:02:43.407 --> 00:02:46.969
When you first check into the Ice Hotel, you must change your clothes.

36
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:53.479
Guests are supplied with a specially made sleeping bag, warm clothes, thermal underwear, and gloves.

37
00:02:54.199 --> 00:02:58.703
You sure need themㅡthe average temperature inside is -5℃.

38
00:02:59.464 --> 00:03:02.907
That's a lot warmer than the -40℃ outside!

39
00:03:03.868 --> 00:03:08.032
After seeing a movie in the ice cinema and having a drink in an ice glass at the

40
00:03:08.112 --> 00:03:10.855
ice bar, you can go to sleep on your ice bed.

41
00:03:11.675 --> 00:03:12.777
How do you sleep on ice?

42
00:03:13.357 --> 00:03:19.134
Well, your bed, which is mostly a block of ice, also consists of a wooden plank, a foam

43
00:03:19.174 --> 00:03:21.756
mattress, sheets, and reindeer skins.

44
00:03:22.677 --> 00:03:27.021
Only the toilets of the hotel, housed in a separate building, are not made of ice.

45
00:03:27.561 --> 00:03:32.225
Phew! In April, the sun's rays start to melt the Ice Hotel.

46
00:03:32.906 --> 00:03:38.330
The last guests check out, and the Ice Hotel slowly but surely becomes a "water hotel."

47
00:03:39.231 --> 00:03:42.814
All the water flows back into the Torne River, from where it came.

48
00:03:43.975 --> 00:03:50.387
The Wieliczka Salt Mine There are other buildings that look similar to the Ice Hotel, but thankfully, much

49
00:03:50.447 --> 00:03:54.110
warmerㅡthe salt buildings inside the Wieliczka salt mine.

50
00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:59.213
The Wieliczka salt mine near Krakow, Poland, is a vast underground city.

51
00:04:00.154 --> 00:04:05.617
It is over seven hundred years old and consists of nine levels with over two thousand chambers.

52
00:04:06.458 --> 00:04:08.179
How did this hidden city come to be?

53
00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:15.344
According to a thirteenth-century legend, Princess Kinga of Hungary was to be married to King Boleslaus of Poland.

54
00:04:16.192 --> 00:04:20.134
Kinga asked her father for a precious gift to give to her fiance.

55
00:04:20.915 --> 00:04:25.237
The people in Poland had everythingㅡwhat could she give that was special and unique?

56
00:04:26.138 --> 00:04:31.821
One thing that Polish people could not get easily was salt; Poland had to import salt from its

57
00:04:31.861 --> 00:04:36.083
neighbors. Back then, salt was indispensable for survival.

58
00:04:36.964 --> 00:04:42.667
It was used for the preservation of meat, butter, and fish; in leather production; and later, for the

59
00:04:42.727 --> 00:04:43.888
production of gunpowder.

60
00:04:44.976 --> 00:04:49.019
So, Kinga asked her father for a Hungarian salt mine, and he agreed.

61
00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:56.364
When Princess Kinga visited the salt mine her father had given her, she did a strange thingㅡshe threw

62
00:04:56.424 --> 00:04:58.126
her engagement ring into the tunnel!

63
00:04:58.986 --> 00:05:02.789
The princess' servants were shocked, but Princess Kinga was calm.

64
00:05:03.749 --> 00:05:07.172
She left the mine and got ready to meet her husband-to-be in Poland.

65
00:05:08.173 --> 00:05:15.190
After Kinga married King Boleslaus, the princess traveled to Wieliczka, a small town near Krakow in Poland.

66
00:05:15.831 --> 00:05:18.694
She instructed the townspeople to dig a deep hole.

67
00:05:19.574 --> 00:05:22.317
They did, and were overjoyed to discover salt.

68
00:05:23.078 --> 00:05:27.562
Finding salt back then was the equivalent of finding oil, or even gold, now.

69
00:05:28.443 --> 00:05:35.370
When the diggers looked at the first block of salt carefully, they discovered something elseㅡKinga's engagement ring was

70
00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:37.372
inside. It was a miracle.

71
00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:45.244
After her death, Princess Kinga was declared the patron saint of salt miners, and the Wieliczka salt mine

72
00:05:45.304 --> 00:05:47.306
became the largest salt mine in Europe.

73
00:05:48.406 --> 00:05:53.669
By the fourteenth century, the salt from Wieliczka was generating one-third of Poland's income.

74
00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.911
But how did a salt mine become a salt city?

75
00:05:57.912 --> 00:06:04.676
Mining can be a hard and lonely jobㅡimagine spending hours underground, slowly digging into the salt, bit by

76
00:06:04.736 --> 00:06:10.423
bit. One day, a miner decided not to dig, but to chip away at the walls, and let

77
00:06:10.443 --> 00:06:11.244
a shape come out.

78
00:06:12.144 --> 00:06:14.846
The result was a small but beautiful sculpture.

79
00:06:15.827 --> 00:06:18.789
Slowly, other miners started to carve the salt, too.

80
00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:23.953
They made sculptures and engravings in the salt, and the mine managers encouraged them.

81
00:06:25.033 --> 00:06:32.639
Years passed, and the miners' projects became more ambitiousㅡlike the Chapel of the Blessed Kinga, which is the

82
00:06:32.699 --> 00:06:34.420
same size as a regular church.

83
00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.722
It is 50 meters long and 12 meters high.

84
00:06:38.422 --> 00:06:46.267
Two things make it unusual: first, it is one hundred meters underground, and second, the altar, pews, religious

85
00:06:46.287 --> 00:06:50.949
statues, and even the chandeliers that hang from the ceiling are all made of salt.

86
00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:58.274
Mass is still celebrated here three times a year: on Christmas Day, Miner's Day, and Blessed Kinga Day.

87
00:06:59.114 --> 00:07:04.401
The salt miners did not stop with an underground church; you can also eat lunch in the mine's

88
00:07:04.481 --> 00:07:10.144
restaurant, see a movie in the mine's cinema, cross a bridge over an underground lake, and even send

89
00:07:10.204 --> 00:07:12.205
a postcard from the mine's post office!

90
00:07:13.006 --> 00:07:19.329
The Wieliczka salt mine is now a UNESCO World Heritage site, which means the artwork and creativity of

91
00:07:19.369 --> 00:07:21.730
the salt miners will be preserved forever.

92
00:07:23.051 --> 00:07:29.815
Bottle Houses There is another unique kind of building that uses a material that is cheap, sturdy, and

93
00:07:29.895 --> 00:07:30.655
easy to find.

94
00:07:31.176 --> 00:07:31.656
What is it?

95
00:07:32.284 --> 00:07:33.244
The humble bottle.

96
00:07:34.105 --> 00:07:40.106
The first bottle house was built in 1902 in Nevada, the United States, during a gold-mining boom.

97
00:07:40.747 --> 00:07:44.668
Gold miners lived in desert camps, and building materials were scarce.

98
00:07:45.668 --> 00:07:51.770
Freight trains often carried supplies of liquor to these camps before bringing other materials, such as wood, cement,

99
00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:52.450
and tools.

100
00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:59.332
Saloons were among the first commercial buildings in the mining camps, so there was no shortage of bottles!

101
00:08:00.128 --> 00:08:05.470
Before long, garbage dumps filled with beer, wine, and liquor bottles surrounded these boomtowns.

102
00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:11.891
Some of the gold miners got sick of living in tents and, with no other building materials available,

103
00:08:12.312 --> 00:08:16.233
decided to use a material that was plentifulㅡthe empty bottles.

104
00:08:17.233 --> 00:08:21.574
Miners set the bottles in adobe or concrete and built themselves homes.

105
00:08:22.695 --> 00:08:27.276
One house in Tonopah, Nevada, was made from over ten thousand bottles.

106
00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:32.142
For the miners, the best thing about bottles was their costㅡnothing.

107
00:08:32.942 --> 00:08:38.304
As long as the miners kept going to the local saloons, they could keep building their bottle houses.

108
00:08:39.225 --> 00:08:46.968
Nowadays, people build bottle houses for environmental reasonsㅡwhy use new materials when you can reuse and recycle?

109
00:08:47.728 --> 00:08:55.491
In September 2003, Brazilian bar owner Celio de Oliveira began to collect discarded plastic soda bottles from a

110
00:08:55.551 --> 00:08:56.912
beach in Rio de Janeiro.

111
00:08:57.672 --> 00:09:01.055
"My main goal was to clean the beach," he later recalled.

112
00:09:01.075 --> 00:09:07.319
"Then I had the idea to build a house using them, and all of a sudden, I had

113
00:09:07.439 --> 00:09:08.620
10,000 bottles."

114
00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:15.345
The result was a home built from nothing but bottles; even its light switches are made from bottle

115
00:09:15.365 --> 00:09:23.270
tops. In Ibiza, Spain, a local environmental group called Greenheart made an eco-office in a bottle house, with

116
00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:25.652
computers powered by the sun and the wind.

117
00:09:26.472 --> 00:09:30.535
This bottle house is completely made from recycled and natural materials.

118
00:09:31.635 --> 00:09:36.998
The bottles are joined together with the red soil of Ibiza and strengthened with straw to make walls.

119
00:09:38.039 --> 00:09:42.041
The roof is made from a satellite dish and the top of an old bus shelter.

120
00:09:42.902 --> 00:09:48.625
The windows came from many placesㅡone window is from a car, and there is even a window from

121
00:09:48.665 --> 00:09:49.506
a washing machine.

122
00:09:50.346 --> 00:09:56.491
By using a recycled material like bottles, people can build homes that are both unique and kind to

123
00:09:56.531 --> 00:09:57.171
the environment.

124
00:09:58.012 --> 00:10:05.298
Whether they are built from snow, salt, or recycled bottles, these amazing buildings show how resourceful and inventive

125
00:10:05.358 --> 00:10:07.920
people can create shelter from the things around them.
